advisory; a second or third is appropriate only for independent, current-activity
risks that matter now. Keep open/deferred architecture reminders out of the way
until their milestone.

That is deliberately open, and it is wider than "what is wrong here". It includes a simpler way to
do what they are already doing; something they appear not to have considered; an assumption worth
testing before it is built on; a place this design will be awkward to extend later; an existing
piece of the codebase they seem not to know about; a different approach that would fit better; or
something they have got right that is worth preserving as the code changes around it.

Defects are part of that range, not the whole of it. A colleague who only ever pointed out faults
would be using half the channel — and would be easy to stop listening to.

